WEBVTT

00:00:00.950 --> 00:00:03.919
ekai builds an AI twin that lives inside Slack,

00:00:03.919 --> 00:00:08.025
and answers your team’s questions using context you already have.

00:00:08.075 --> 00:00:09.325
The product worked.

00:00:09.375 --> 00:00:11.458
The website didn’t say so.

00:00:12.708 --> 00:00:16.876
On the old site, the main line talked about saving time on repetitive work,

00:00:16.876 --> 00:00:19.376
and never said what the product actually was.

00:00:20.226 --> 00:00:21.314
The numbers agreed.

00:00:21.364 --> 00:00:24.689
63 percent of mobile visitors bounced.

00:00:24.739 --> 00:00:27.564
The average visit lasted twelve seconds.

00:00:27.614 --> 00:00:30.826
Fewer than two percent touched the call to action.

00:00:30.876 --> 00:00:34.406
And fewer than half a percent ever reached the features.

00:00:35.656 --> 00:00:39.643
Keerthi worked directly with the founders, as the product designer.

00:00:39.693 --> 00:00:43.268
He took the website from research all the way to final motion.

00:00:43.318 --> 00:00:47.238
And he designed the platform flows and the full Chrome extension,

00:00:47.238 --> 00:00:48.444
which are under NDA.

00:00:49.694 --> 00:00:52.619
He started with conversations, not analytics.

00:00:52.669 --> 00:00:55.119
They revealed more than the data could.

00:00:55.169 --> 00:00:58.256
People weren’t just unclear about what the product did.

00:00:58.306 --> 00:01:02.026
They weren’t sure they could trust an AI inside their daily work.

00:01:02.876 --> 00:01:06.751
Reports on AI adoption kept listing the same blockers.

00:01:06.801 --> 00:01:08.101
Is the value clear?

00:01:08.151 --> 00:01:10.201
How much effort is setup?

00:01:10.251 --> 00:01:11.651
And where does our data go?

00:01:11.701 --> 00:01:15.904
Those three questions became the spine of every decision.

00:01:17.154 --> 00:01:22.169
So the page was rebuilt in the order a skeptical buyer asks questions: what it is,

00:01:22.169 --> 00:01:27.062
real examples, integrations, proof, security, and only then, the call to action.

00:01:28.312 --> 00:01:31.175
He explored three directions and scored them.

00:01:31.225 --> 00:01:37.312
Concept B, a modular backbone, scored highest, but it lacked an emotional opener.

00:01:37.362 --> 00:01:42.636
So the final design is a hybrid: B’s structure, with A’s narrative hero.

00:01:43.486 --> 00:01:46.494
The visual language moved toward trust:

00:01:46.494 --> 00:01:50.658
orange for energy and warmth, teal for trust and calm.

00:01:51.908 --> 00:01:53.658
And the sections came alive.

00:01:53.708 --> 00:01:56.558
The hero plays a simple Slack-like conversation,

00:01:56.558 --> 00:01:59.704
so you see what the twin does before you read a word.

00:02:00.554 --> 00:02:04.316
Short feature clips explain each part of the product in seconds.

00:02:04.366 --> 00:02:09.670
And security is explained in plain language, with no jargon and no overclaiming.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.756
The redesigned site shipped, and it’s live today,

00:02:13.756 --> 00:02:18.212
with a mobile layout designed for phones instead of shrunk down from desktop.

00:02:19.462 --> 00:02:22.638
What this project taught him is that in AI products,

00:02:22.638 --> 00:02:24.837
clarity and honesty are the product.

00:02:24.887 --> 00:02:27.424
The technology was already strong.

00:02:27.475 --> 00:02:32.322
The job was to make it understandable, trustworthy, and easy to act on.

00:02:33.172 --> 00:02:35.160
Design by Keerthi Vardhan.

